Description: Fix CVE-2021-4104 — disable JMSAppender JNDI lookups by default
 JMSAppender.activateOptions() performed JNDI InitialContext lookups on
 attacker-controllable configuration values (TopicConnectionFactoryBindingName,
 TopicBindingName, InitialContextFactoryName, ProviderURL). An attacker with
 write access to the log4j configuration could point these at a malicious
 JNDI/RMI/LDAP provider, causing remote object loading and deserialization
 (CWE-502 / JNDI injection) leading to remote code execution, similar to
 CVE-2021-44228.
 .
 This patch makes JMSAppender secure-by-default: all JNDI activity is gated
 behind an explicit opt-in system property
 (org.apache.log4j.net.JMSAppender.enableJndiLookup=true). When the property is
 not set to "true", activateOptions() logs an error and returns before creating
 any InitialContext or performing any lookup, so the vulnerable deserialization
 path is never reached and the appender stays inert (topicConnection remains
 null). Legitimate deployments may deliberately re-enable JNDI via the property.
Origin: ai-synthesized, CVE-2021-4104
Author: Root.io Novel Patcher <security@root.io>
Last-Update: 2026-09-24

